# AGENTS.md

For AI coding agents in this nautilus (IEC 61131-3) project. Programs are
**text**; the VS Code extension draws the ladder, FBD and SFC diagrams from
it with a computed layout, so you never place anything, and the structure
of the text alone decides whether the diagram is readable. A program that
compiles can still draw as a mess: follow these rules.
Docs: https://nautilus.joyautomation.com

## Check every change

```sh
naut check            # compile every program; prints errors and warnings
naut test             # acceptance tests (*_test.yaml), in virtual time
naut test -run fill   # only tests whose name matches a regex
naut test -list       # list the tests without running them
naut hmi build        # build the operator screen alone, and say what's wrong
naut run              # optional: the screen, dashboard and tag API on http://localhost:8080
```

- Run `naut check` after every edit. Treat a warning as a bug: "local X
  shadows the project tag X" and the like mean the logic is not doing what
  it looks like.
- Warnings with an ID (`LD001`, `FBD002`, `SFC001`) are the diagram rules
  below, checked. Fix the diagram; never add a `(* @allow ID: reason *)`
  pragma to silence one unless a person asked for it.
- `naut check` does not run the logic. When behavior changes, add or change
  a test in `*_test.yaml` and run `naut test`.

## Project layout

| Path | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| `nautilus.yaml` | tasks (one program per task, each with a scan), tags, driver, alarms |
| `*.st` `*.ld` `*.fbd` `*.sfc` in the root | one `PROGRAM` per file; each is a task's program |
| `lib/**` (or a root file with no `PROGRAM`) | `FUNCTION_BLOCK`s, `FUNCTION`s and `TYPE`s, usable from every program |
| `*_test.yaml` | acceptance tests; the fixture is `nautilus.yaml` itself |
| `tags/*.yaml` | more tags, listed under `tag-files:` in the manifest |

The first task in `tasks:` is the main task. Programs share one tag store.

## Tags

```yaml
tags:
  - { name: LT101_Level, role: input,    type: REAL, init: 0.0,  unit: "%", desc: "Tank level (LT-101)" }
  - { name: FullSP,      role: setpoint, type: REAL, init: 80.0, unit: "%", desc: "Fill stops here" }
  - { name: XV101_Cmd,   role: output,   type: BOOL, init: false, desc: "Fill valve XV-101" }
```

- Roles: `input` (driver writes), `output` (logic writes, driver sends),
  `setpoint` (operator writes), `state` (logic writes, nothing sends).
- Give **every** tag an `init:`, so it starts at a value you chose. Without
  one, a typed `setpoint` or `state` tag starts at zero, but an `output`
  (or a local-I/O `input` before its first reading) holds nothing until it
  is written, and reading it first faults the scan with "undefined tag". A
  seal-in reads its own coil before writing it. `naut check` reports an
  output read before it is written: an error, or a warning where it cannot
  be sure.
- Give every tag a `desc:`. The ladder editor prints it under each contact
  and coil; FBD and SFC show it on hover.
- Manifest tags are in scope in every program. Do **not** redeclare them in
  `VAR`: that makes a private copy that shadows the tag. Use `VAR` only for
  block instances (timers, counters, your blocks) and true scratch values.
- Each tag has **one writer**: one program, one rung or statement. Any
  number of programs may read it.
- Thresholds an operator might change are `setpoint` tags, not literals:
  `GE(LT101_Level, HighLevelSP)`, not `GT(LT101_Level, 90.0)`.
- Names: `LT101_Level`, `HeatPermissive` for tags; `hhTmr`, `tic` for
  instances; `fillcmd` for rungs. Never `r1`, `w1`, `S1`.

## Pick the language by the logic

| Logic | Language |
| --- | --- |
| interlocks, permissives, seal-ins, alarm/horn logic, mode selection | LD |
| analog signal flow: scaling, PID loops, limits, selection, filters | FBD |
| phases and sequences: fill, heat, drain; startup/shutdown; batch | SFC |
| math, loops, arrays, `IF`/`CASE`, strings, simulation | ST |

Split a machine across programs (one per task) rather than forcing one
language: the SFC **asks** (`FillReq`), the LD **decides**
(`FillReq /LSHH101 ( XV101_Cmd )`), the FBD computes analog values. Asked
for "ladder" for mostly math, put the math in FBD or ST and say so.

## Ladder (`.ld`)

```
PROGRAM Interlocks
(* Turns the sequence's requests into commands, or refuses them. *)
LD
  RUNG fillcmd (* fill only while asked and the high-high switch is clear *)
    FillReq /LSHH101 ( XV101_Cmd )

  RUNG draincmd (* draining is always allowed *)
    DrainReq ( XV102_Cmd )

  RUNG heatperm (* the heater needs liquid over it and no trip *)
    GE(LT101_Level, HeatMinLevelSP) /HeaterTrip ( HeatPermissive )

  RUNG hhalarm (* LSHH-101 held for 2 s *)
    LSHH101 hhTmr:TON(PT := T#2S) ( HighHighAlm )

  RUNG horn (* a new alarm sounds the horn; it seals in until acknowledged *)
    [ +HighHighAlm | +HighLevelAlm | Horn ] /HornAck ( Horn )
END_LD
END_PROGRAM
```

Elements: `X` NO contact, `/X` NC contact, `+X` / `-X` rising / falling
edge, `[ a b | c ]` parallel branch (legs are series), `GT(a, b)` function
contact (any comparison), `inst:TON(PT := T#5S)` block in the rung,
`{ Dest := Src }` move, `( X )` coil, `( S X )` / `( R X )` set / reset,
`( P X )` / `( N X )` edge coil. `// text` on its own line is a note.

1. **One rung, one idea, one output.** Several coils on one rung only when
   they really share the whole condition.
2. **Read left to right**: request or mode, then permissives (usually NC
   contacts), then the coil at the right. Seal-in: `[ Start | Run ] /Stop
   ( Run )`.
3. **Keep rungs small**: at most 6 elements in series (LD003), and no branch
   inside a branch (`[ a [ b | c ] | d ]`, LD002). Past that, compute a named
   intermediate BOOL on its own rung (`PumpPermissive`, `PumpAutoCall`) and
   use it as one contact.
4. **Factor, don't repeat**: a contact on every leg of a branch belongs once,
   in series, outside the branch (LD004).
5. **Name and comment every rung**: a real name, plus `(* … *)` saying
   *why*, not restating the contacts (LD005).
6. **One coil per BOOL.** Drive a BOOL with `( X )` on exactly one rung. A
   latch is a `( S X )` / `( R X )` pair; never mix those with `( X )` on the
   same tag (LD001).
7. **Power is boolean.** Comparisons are contacts; `ADD(...)` is not. Numeric
   work goes inside a comparison's arguments or a `{ }` move. More than one
   short assignment means the logic belongs in FBD or ST. Compare against a
   setpoint tag or a named constant, not a number: `GE(Level, HighLevelSP)`,
   not `GE(Level, 90.0)` (LD006).
8. **Timers and counters sit in the rung**: power drives `IN` (`CU`, `CD`,
   `CLK`); never pass it yourself. Capture values with `=>`:
   `c1:CTU(PV := 9999, CV => Starts)`.
9. **Use edge contacts** (`+X`) instead of hand-built one-shot latches.
10. **Repeated equipment** (two pumps) is a `FUNCTION_BLOCK` with rungs in
    `lib/*.ld`, instantiated once per unit in a rung.
11. **Not supported**: jumps/labels, MCR zones, negated coils `( /X )`, coils
    inside a branch or before a block, vendor mnemonics (`XIC`, `OTE`,
    `GRT`, `.DN`). ASCII-art ladder is not a file format.

## Function Block Diagram (`.fbd`)

```
PROGRAM Control
(* TIC-101 heater loop and the high level alarm. *)
FBD
  NETWORK 'TIC-101: heater temperature loop'
  // reverse acting (DIRECT := FALSE); off auto, CV follows CV_MAN (0)
  heatOn = AND(HeatReq, HeatPermissive)
  tic : PID(AUTO := heatOn, PV := TT101_Temp, SP := TempSP,
            KP := TicKp, KI := TicKi, CV_MIN := 0.0, CV_MAX := 100.0,
            DIRECT := FALSE)
  E101_Out := tic.CV

  NETWORK 'LAH-101: high level, 10 s on-delay'
  hiLevel = GE(LT101_Level, HighLevelSP)
  hlTmr : TON(IN := hiLevel, PT := T#10S)
  HighLevelAlm := hlTmr.Q
END_FBD
END_PROGRAM
```

Forms: `wire = BLOCK(...)` names a block output; `Tag := expr` is a coil
(writes a variable); `inst : TYPE(Pin := ...)` declares and calls a block;
`inst.Pin` reads an output; `NOT x` negates a pin; `NETWORK 'title'` starts
a network.

1. **One `NETWORK 'title'` per function**: one loop, one alarm, one scaling.
   Each draws as its own titled band (FBD002).
2. **One block per line.** Give each intermediate result a wire named for
   what it carries (`hiLevel`, `err`, `heatOn`), not `w1`. Nest calls at
   most two deep (FBD001); a long nested expression draws as a wide tree of
   crossing wires.
3. **Write in signal order**: wires from inputs, then block calls, then the
   coils that write tags.
4. **Compare once, reuse the wire.** One variable feeding many blocks in a
   network draws long crossing wires; one named comparison does not. Compare
   against a setpoint tag or a named constant, not a number (FBD003).
5. **Use the library block** (`PID`, `LIMIT`, `SEL`, `TON`, `R_TRIG`, `CTU`,
   `AND`/`OR` with several inputs) instead of rebuilding it from math, and
   do not add blocks for what the block already does.
6. **No operators and no control flow.** Write `ADD(a, b)`, `GT(a, b)`,
   `AND(a, b, c)`, never `a + b` or `a > b` (the parser rejects them: "FBD
   has no operators"). No `IF`, `CASE` or loops: that is ST.
7. **Feedback goes through a variable**: a seal-in is
   `Alm := AND(OR(set, Alm), NOT clear)`. A wire that reads itself is an
   error.
8. **Never write a `(* @layout … *)` block.** It holds positions a person
   dragged in the editor. Do not invent or edit coordinates; leave an
   existing block alone.

## Sequential Function Chart (`.sfc`)

```
PROGRAM Sequence
(* Batch: fill, heat, drain. The chart only asks (FillReq, HeatReq,
   DrainReq); interlocks.ld decides whether the equipment runs. *)
SFC
  INITIAL_STEP Idle:
  END_STEP

  STEP Fill (MAXTIME := T#10M, ERROR := FillOverrun):
    N FillReq;
  END_STEP

  STEP Heat:
    N HeatReq;
  END_STEP

  STEP Drain:
    N DrainReq;
    P0 CountBatch;  (* once, as Drain ends *)
  END_STEP

  TRANSITION t_start FROM Idle TO Fill := Start;
  END_TRANSITION

  (* abort first: on a shared source step, declaration order is priority *)
  TRANSITION t_fill_abort FROM Fill TO Drain := Abort OR Fill.ERR;
  END_TRANSITION
  TRANSITION t_full FROM Fill TO Heat := LT101_Level >= FullSP;
  END_TRANSITION

  TRANSITION t_heat_abort FROM Heat TO Drain := Abort;
  END_TRANSITION
  TRANSITION t_hot FROM Heat TO Drain := TT101_Temp >= TempSP AND Heat.T >= T#30S;
  END_TRANSITION

  TRANSITION t_empty FROM Drain TO Idle := LT101_Level <= EmptySP;
  END_TRANSITION

  (* by convention, ACTION bodies go last, before END_SFC *)
  ACTION CountBatch:
    BatchCount := BatchCount + 1;
  END_ACTION
END_SFC
END_PROGRAM
```

1. **Steps are process states** (`Fill`, `Heat`), not `S1`, `S2` (SFC002).
   Transitions get names too (`t_full`).
2. **The condition lives in the transition**, written in process terms. Never
   keep a `State` integer that actions set and transitions compare: the
   chart then shows `State = 2` and hides the logic (SFC001).
3. **Drive outputs with associations** (`N FillReq;`). Use an `ACTION`
   body (ST) only for what an association cannot do, such as a counter
   (`P0 CountBatch;` above); keep it short, with no `IF` deciding where the
   chart goes next. `ACTION name: … END_ACTION` blocks go inside the chart;
   put them after the transitions, so the chart reads steps first.
4. **One `INITIAL_STEP`**, a safe idle with no outputs. Every step reachable.
5. **Alternatives**: transitions sharing a source fire by declaration order,
   so declare abort and fault paths first.
6. **Time**: `Step.T >= T#30S` in a condition; supervise a step with
   `(MAXTIME := T#10M, ERROR := Tag)` and route `Step.ERR` to an abort.
7. **Parallel branches**: `TO (A, B)` splits, `FROM (A, B)` joins.
8. Qualifiers: `N`, `S`, `R`, `P1`, `P0`, `P`, and timed `L`, `D`, `SD`,
   `DS`, `SL` (`D Vent(T#3S);`). Several steps may `N` the same BOOL; they
   OR together.

**ST** is standard IEC: a `FUNCTION_BLOCK` per piece of equipment in
`lib/`, `T#5S` time literals, single-quoted strings, IEC names only.

## Operator screens

naut builds the operator screen from the project itself, with no npm, and
`naut run` serves it at `/` (the engineering dashboard moves to
`/_nautilus/`). Guide: https://nautilus.joyautomation.com/guides/operator-screens/

- A `*.mimic.json` is a screen: equipment placed on a canvas, pipes between
  it, tags bound to it. A project with mimics gets a page for each. Use
  the kit's equipment (`Tank`, `Pump`, `Valve`, `Gauge`, `CommandButton`,
  `WriteToggle`, ...) and a `{Name}.svelte` of your own where the kit
  doesn't draw what you need, with its pipe ports in `{Name}.component.json`.
- What repeats (four filters, eight pumps) goes in the mimic's `units`,
  drawn once: `{n}` in any id, tag, label or pipe end becomes each value,
  and each copy moves by `step`:
  `"units": [{"id": "filter", "each": [1, 2, 3, 4], "step": [420, 0],
  "equipment": [{"id": "F{n}", "component": "FilterCell", "x": 230, "y": 370,
  "bind": {"level": "F{n}_Level"}}], "pipes": [...], "labels": [...]}]`.
- naut is the only tool a project needs, and often the only one there is
  (the playground has no Python or Node). Write every file by hand, never
  a script that writes one: `units` (or `{#each}` in an app) is how a
  screen repeats itself.
- For more than mimics, write an app: `hmi/src/routes/+page.svelte` is `/`,
  components go in `hmi/src/lib`, and `server.hmi: hmi` in the manifest.
  It is SvelteKit's layout, client-side; Go is the only backend.
- Never hand-write an HTML or JavaScript page for the operator screen, and
  never point `server.hmi` at one: naut doesn't check it, it can't use the
  kit, and the editor can't show it.
- `naut check` checks screens: every tag a binding, a label or a control
  names, every pipe's ports, and the screen builds. A screen imports only
  Svelte, `@joyautomation/nautilus-hmi`, `@joyautomation/nautilus-hmi-3d`,
  three and Threlte.
- With Chrome, Chromium or Edge on the machine, `naut check` also opens
  each page at desktop and phone size and reports what it sees: a page
  that throws or is blank, a pipe that draws nothing, a shape drawn far
  outside its drawing, sideways scroll on a phone. Fix them like compile
  errors. A screen you haven't seen rendered isn't done.
- In a component that uses `$state(...)`, never name a prop or variable
  `state`: Svelte then reads `$state` as a store subscription and the
  component throws when it mounts. Name it for what it holds
  (`filterState`).

## Tests (`*_test.yaml`)

```yaml
tests:
  - name: high-high switch closes the fill valve
    given: { Start: true, LSHH101: true }
    scans: 2
    expect: { XV101_Cmd: false }

  - name: high-high alarm waits its 2 s
    given: { LSHH101: true }
    steps:
      - advance: 1.9s
        expect: { HighHighAlm: false }
      - advance: 0.2s
        expect: { HighHighAlm: true, Horn: true }
```

Time is virtual: `advance: 10s` runs ten seconds exactly, instantly. A step
takes one of `scans`, `advance`, `until`; `expect` checks its end, `always`
every scan, and both take ST expressions (`- E101_Out = 0.0`) too. Test
every timer, permissive and abort path.

Write the tests from the specification, not from the code: every sentence
that says the control sets, clears, holds, waits for or times something
gets a test that checks it, including a command the control must clear
once it has taken it.

## Before you finish

- `naut check`: no errors, no new warnings (the screen builds). `naut test`
  passes.
- New or changed behavior has a test in `*_test.yaml`: every timer,
  permissive and abort path.
- Every rung and network has a comment; every step and transition a name.
- Rungs of about six elements at most, FBD nesting two deep at most, one
  writer per tag, no manifest tag redeclared in `VAR`, no `(* @layout *)`
  added or changed.
